            <ArticleTitle>Albendazole treatment for Giardia intestinalis infections in school children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>78-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A randomized controlled trial, 113 school children with Giardia intestinalis infection were treated with albendazole or tinidazole. Albendazole 400 mg once a day x 3 days and tinidazole 50 mg/kg single dose were given orally to 62 and 51 children, respectively. Parasitological cure was documented when there were &gt; or = 2 times negative stool examination for G. intestinalis at 1-2 weeks after therapy. Thirty-one of 62 (50%) children treated with albendazole and 49 of 51 (96.1%) children treated with tinidazole had parasitological cure (p &lt; 0.001). No major side effects were observed except one case in tinidazole group had severe headache for 30 hours. Albendazole appears to be safe and produced a moderate cure rate for G. intestinalis infection when a 3 day anthelmintic regimen is given.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
